---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'; import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro'; import { site } from '../config/site';
---
<BaseLayout title="Privacy and Data Handling | FM AI Assistent" description="Learn what the static FM AI Assistent website collects, what the installed app processes locally and what selected AI providers receive." path="/privacy/">
  <div class="page-shell"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Privacy & data'}]} /><header class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Three separate boundaries</p><h1>Privacy and data handling.</h1><p class="lead">The public website, the installed application and your selected AI provider do different jobs. This page separates them clearly.</p></header>
    <section class="section content-grid"><article class="prose"><h2>1. This static website</h2><p>This marketing and information site is built as static files on GitHub Pages. It has no account system, forms, database, analytics, advertising scripts, tracking cookies or embedded chat widget. It does not ask for or process your FM save, passwords or API keys.</p><p>GitHub Pages and the networks used to reach it may process ordinary web-request data under their own terms. This project does not add a separate visitor-tracking layer.</p><h2>2. The installed local application</h2><p>FM AI Assistent reads available FM26 data from the running process when you request a load or refresh. It stores an application snapshot locally for browsing and analysis. Tactic files are decoded locally. The application listens on a loopback address by default; keep it local.</p><p>The current documentation says an OpenRouter API key and conversations are held only for the backend session, are not stored in the application database, browser storage or logs, and are cleared on disconnect, session expiry or shutdown.</p><h2>3. AI provider communication</h2><p>When you use an AI agent, your messages, enabled managed-club/tactic/snapshot context and tool results required for the response reach the provider you selected. Depending on the route, this can include model providers used by OpenRouter. Provider terms, retention, account settings, privacy restrictions, usage limits and costs apply.</p><p>Codex, Antigravity and GitHub Copilot agents may also have approved access to local files or tools according to their own permission model. Review each prompt before allowing access.</p><h2>Your practical choices</h2><ul><li>Browse the loaded database without starting an AI conversation.</li><li>Disable managed-club or tactic context before sending a message.</li><li>Use only providers and models whose terms you accept.</li><li>Disconnect OpenRouter or stop a turn when you no longer want requests made.</li><li>Keep the app’s loopback service off public networks.</li></ul><h2>Changes and questions</h2><p>Feature behavior can change between releases. Check the current <a href={`${site.repository}#privacy-and-safety`} rel="noopener noreferrer">repository documentation</a> and release notes before relying on this summary. Report a discrepancy through <a href={site.issues} rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Issues</a>.</p><p class="notice">This page explains current project behavior and is not legal advice or a claim of independent compliance certification.</p></article><aside class="info-card"><h3>At a glance</h3><p><strong>Website:</strong> no project-added analytics or input forms.</p><p><strong>Local app:</strong> save snapshot and tactic processing on your computer.</p><p><strong>AI use:</strong> messages and enabled context go to the selected external provider.</p></aside></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
